			# notes 
				A -> 10 -> 1010
				
				B -> 11 -> 1011

				C -> 12 -> 1100

				D -> 13 -> 1101
					     
 				E -> 14 -> 1110 

				F -> 15 -> 1111
		
		* why invert the 7th bit
		
			- Mac address can be divided into two types

				# UAA (Universally Administered Address)
					-> Uniquely assigned to the device by the manufacturer

				#  LAA (Locally Administered Address)
					-> Manually assigned by an admin (with the mac-address command on the interface) or protocol. 
					   Not Globaly unique

				# You can identify a UAA or LAA by the 7th bit of the mac address, called the U/L bit
					
					* U = Universal	| * L = Local

					-> U/L bit set to 0 = UAA
					-> U/L bit set to 1 = LAA

				# In the context of IPv6 address / EUI-64, the meaning of the U/L bit is reserved

					-> U/L bit set to 0 = Mac Address EUI-64 interface ID was made from an LAA

					-> U/L bit set to 1 = Mac Address EUI-64 interface ID was made from an UAA

IPv6 address Types :

		* Global Unicast Addresses

			-> Public Addresses which can be used over the internet

			-> They must be globally unique

				# Must be registered to the IANA
			
			-> originally defined as rhe 2000::/3 block (2000:: to 3FFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF)
			
			-> Now defined as all addresses which arent reserved for other purposes

				ex :
					2001:0DB8:8B00|:0001:0000:0000:0000:0001 / 64
						      |	  |		|	
		    48 bit 'global routing prefix'<---|   |  		|
				      			  |		|
			  16 bit 'subnet identifier'<-----|	 	|
									|-----> 64 bit 'interface identifier'

		* Unique Local Addresses 

			-> Cannot be used over the internet

			-> Unique Local
				
				-> No need to register them

			-> Uses the address block FC00::/7 (FC00:: to FDFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF)

			-> Later update require the 8th bit to be set to 1, so the first two digits must be FD

			-> Gloabl ID should be unique in order to prevent addresses from overlapping when companies merge

				ex :

					FD45:93AC:8A8F:0001:0000:0000:0000:0001/64
				
					# FD ---> Indicates a unique Local address

					# 45:93AC:8A8F ---> 40 bit 'global ID', which should be randomly generated

					# 0001 ---> 16 bit 'subnet identifier', used by the enterprise to make various subnets
	
					# 0000:0000:0000:0001 ---> 64 bit 'interface identifier', the host portion of the address

		* Link Local addresses 

			-> automatically generated on IPv6-enabled interfaces

			-> uses the address block FE80::/10 (FE80:: to FEBF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF)

			-> Address won't ever start with :
			
				- FE9

				- FEA

				- FEB

				* it will only start with FE8 *

			-> The interface ID is generated using the EUI-64 rules

		* Multicast Addresses

			-> Unicast (One to One)

				* One source to One destination

			-> Broadcast (One to All)

				* One source to all destinations (within the subnet)

			-> Multicast (One To Many)

				* One source to multiple destinations (that have joined the specific multicast group)

				* IPv6 uses range FF00::/8 for multicast (FF00:: to FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF:FFFF)

			-> IPv6 doesnt use broadcast ( no Broadcast Address in IPv6)

			# Examples :
					Purpose 		IPv6 Address		IPv4 Address

				All nodes/host(Broadcast	   FF02::1		 224.0.0.1

					All Routers		   FF02::2		 224.0.0.2

					OSPF Routers		   FF02::5		 224.0.0.5
			
				      OSPF DRs/BDRs		   FF02::6		 224.0.0.6

				      All RIP Routers		   FF02::9		 224.0.0.9

			 	    All EIGRP Routers		   FF02::A		 224.0.0.10

			-> IPv6 defines multiple multicast 'scopes' 

				* Scopes indicate how far the packet should be forwarded

			-> IPv6 multicast scopes :

				* Inteface Local (FF01) 

					-> Packet doesn't leave the local device

					-> Used to send traffic to a service within the local device

				* LINK Local (FF02)

					-> Packet remains in the local subnet

					-> Routers will not route the packet between subnets

				* Site Local (FF05)

					-> Packets can be forwarded by routers

					-> should be limited to a single physical location (not forwarded over wan)

				* Organizaation Local (FF08)

					-> Wider in scope than site-local (an entire company/organization)

				* Global (FF0E)

					-> No boundaries.

					-> Can be routed over the internet
			
		* Anycast 

			-> new feature of IPv6

			-> One To One | One To Many

			-> Multiple router are configured with the same Ipv6 address

				-> Use routing protocol to advertise the address
				
				-> host send packets to that destination address, routers will forward it to the nearest router configured with 				   that ip address (based on routing metric)

				-> No specific address range for anycast addresses

		* Other IPv6 Addresses 

			-> :: = The unspecified Ipv6 address

				- can be used when a device doesnt yet know its IPv6 address

				- IPv6 default route are configured to ::/0
			
				- Ipv4 equivalent 0.0.0.0

			-> :: 1 = The Loopback address

				- Used to test the protocol stack on the local device
			
				- Messages sent to this address are proccessed within the local device, but not sent to other devices

				- IPv4 equivalent 127.0.0.0/8 address range

				
					 
